# EXPERIMENTAL: a pilot flock. One LLM pilot per body, any backend, wall-clock time, and
# the members divide the work by talking to each other over the bus. See docs/guides/flock.md.
---
duck: 1
name: flock-hello
description: Two different bodies introduce themselves to each other and confirm they were heard.
author: rok
verbs:
  allow: [report_state, observe, stop, say, move_joints]
  confirm: []
budgets:
  max_steps: 6
  max_minutes: 2
  max_llm_calls: 12
success:
  - You told the others what you are, and at least one of them answered you.
abort_when:
  - Same verb fails 3 times in a row
requires: [report_state]
persona: Brief and practical. This is a radio check between robots, not a performance.
providers: [fake, anthropic, openai, gemini, grok]
flock:
  members: [duck, arm]
  allocation:
    method: pilots
robots:
  duck: microduck:sim2d
  arm: lerobot:mock
---

# Task

You are one of two robots that have just been introduced. Find out who else is here and
make sure they know about you.

## Strategy

1. Read `## Your flock`. It names the other robot and gives its datasheet in the same words
   yours is given in.
2. `tell` everyone one sentence: who you are and what your body can do that theirs cannot.
3. Wait, using `report_state`, until something arrives under "Messages from your flock".
4. When another robot has answered you, `declare_success` and say who answered.
5. If nobody answers after a few checks, `declare_failure` and say so. Do not guess.

## What this is for

Nothing here moves anything, on purpose: it is the smallest honest test that two pilots in
two different bodies can find each other and exchange a fact. `say` is a verb the duck has
and the arm does not, and `move_joints` is one the arm has and the duck does not. Each of
you is handed the part of the allowlist your own body can answer for, so the list of verbs
in your prompt is shorter than the one in this file. That is the mechanism a real
heterogeneous task depends on, and this is where you can see it with no robot at risk.
